/* Notes for s25 — generated by tools/build-notes.js from content/notes. Do not edit by hand. */
PG.notes("l209", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why server-rendered pages need a templating language, introduces EJS (Embedded JavaScript) and res.render, then has the learner build a small Express app that tells visitors whether it is a weekday or the weekend using Date.getDay().</p>\n<h3>The problem EJS solves</h3>\n<ul><li>Goal: take a value that arrives on the server (for example a name from a form's POST body, read via body-parser/req.body) and show it inside an HTML page that is then sent back.</li><li>The flow is: form page -&gt; user submits -&gt; POST route receives req.body -&gt; server somehow injects the value into an HTML file -&gt; rendered page returns to the browser. The 'somehow' step is templating.</li><li>Building the HTML as a string inside res.send works for a one-line reply, but gets unmanageable once the page needs stylesheets, fonts and full markup: structure ends up buried in server code.</li><li>Templating gives separation of concerns: HTML/CSS (structure and style) live in view files, JavaScript (behaviour) stays in the server, so each part can be worked on independently and frontend and backend stay apart.</li></ul>\n<h3>Templating languages and why EJS</h3>\n<ul><li>Many exist: Handlebars, Pug, Jinja (Python), Twig (PHP), EJS. Jinja and Twig belong to other languages.</li><li>EJS is the most common choice for Express/Node and stands for Embedded JavaScript.</li><li>An EJS file is ordinary HTML with the .ejs extension, plus JavaScript fenced in special tags. It can, for instance, contain a for loop that emits one list item per element of an array supplied by the server.</li><li>The server runs the template, executes the embedded JavaScript, and sends the finished plain HTML to the browser.</li></ul>\n<h3>res.render instead of res.send / res.sendFile</h3>\n<ul><li>res.sendFile only serves static files as-is, so it cannot insert values. Templates need res.render.</li><li>Signature: res.render(viewName, dataObject). First argument is the template filename (e.g. 'index.ejs'), second is an optional object of key/value pairs.</li><li>Each key becomes a variable in the template. If you pass { name: req.body.name }, the template can output name. The key names must match on both sides.</li><li>Express looks for templates in a folder named views at the project root by default, so EJS files must live there.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Express 5 resolves the 'ejs' view engine automatically from the file extension as long as the ejs package is installed; no app.set('view engine') is needed when the extension is written out.</li></ul>\n<h3>Output tag &lt;%= %&gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>&lt;%= expression %&gt; evaluates the JavaScript expression and writes its value into the HTML at that spot.</li><li>Used here inside an h1: Hey, it's &lt;%= dayType %&gt;, &lt;%= advice %&gt;!</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> &lt;%= %&gt; HTML-escapes the value, which is the safe default for user-supplied text.</li></ul>\n<h3>Editor support</h3>\n<ul><li>Install the VS Code extension 'EJS language support' (by DigitalBrainStem), the most downloaded one. It adds syntax highlighting for .ejs and autocompletes the tags when you type an opening angle bracket.</li></ul>\n<h3>Project setup used in the exercise</h3>\n<ul><li>Make a project folder, cd into it, run npm init with -y style defaults, then npm i express ejs.</li><li>In package.json set &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; so import syntax works.</li><li>Create a views directory, an index.js at the root, and views/index.ejs. A shell trick: chain commands on one line with a semicolon, e.g. mkdir views; touch index.js; touch views/index.ejs. Using the VS Code UI to create them is equivalent.</li><li>Server skeleton: import express, const app = express(), const port = 3000, app.listen(port, callback) that logs the port.</li></ul>\n<h3>Weekday / weekend logic</h3>\n<ul><li>new Date() gives the current date-time; .getDay() returns an integer 0-6 where 0 is Sunday and 6 is Saturday.</li><li>Declare two let variables with weekday defaults (type and adv). If day === 0 || day === 6, overwrite them with weekend wording. The wording is chosen so the sentence reads naturally ('a weekday' vs 'the weekend').</li><li>Pass them to the template under different key names: { dayType: type, advice: adv }. Using different local names than the keys makes the mapping visible.</li><li>To test the other branch without waiting, temporarily construct a specific date, e.g. new Date('2023-06-24') which is a Saturday (or edit it to a weekday date), save, refresh.</li><li>Debug tip: console.log(day) first to confirm getDay works before wiring it into the if.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Server: choose wording from getDay and render the template</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const today = new Date();\n  const day = today.getDay(); // 0 = Sunday ... 6 = Saturday\n\n  let type = &quot;a weekday&quot;;\n  let adv = &quot;it's time to work hard&quot;;\n\n  if (day === 0 || day === 6) {\n    type = &quot;the weekend&quot;;\n    adv = &quot;it's time to have some fun&quot;;\n  }\n\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n    dayType: type,\n    advice: adv,\n  });\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">views/index.ejs: output tags insert the passed values</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Weekday Warrior&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hey, it's &lt;%= dayType %&gt;, &lt;%= advice %&gt;!&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Setup commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">npm init -y\nnpm i express ejs\nmkdir views; touch index.js; touch views/index.ejs</pre>\n<h2>The task: Weekday or weekend advice page</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty folder. Build the project from scratch (initialise npm, install Express and EJS, create index.js and views/index.ejs).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Visiting http://localhost:3000 shows one h1 that greets the user and says it is a weekday and time to work hard, or the weekend and time to have fun, according to the current day.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a project folder, run npm init, install express and ejs, and set &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json.</li><li>Create a views folder, index.js, and views/index.ejs.</li><li>In index.js import express, create the app, set the port to 3000 and call app.listen with a console.log.</li><li>Add app.get('/') that renders index.ejs and passes an object with two properties (dayType and advice) holding weekday defaults.</li><li>In index.ejs create the HTML boilerplate with an h1 using &lt;%= %&gt; for both values.</li><li>Run the server (node index.js) and check the weekday text appears.</li><li>Look up Date.getDay() in MDN/W3Schools; create new Date(), call getDay(), and console.log it to verify.</li><li>Use an if statement: when the day is 0 or 6 change the type and advice to the weekend wording.</li><li>Test the weekend branch by hard-coding a Saturday date, then restore the real date.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Weekday: 'Hey, it's a weekday, it's time to work hard!' Weekend: 'Hey, it's the weekend, it's time to have some fun!' in an h1.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const day = new Date().getDay();\n  let type = &quot;a weekday&quot;;\n  let adv = &quot;it's time to work hard&quot;;\n  if (day === 0 || day === 6) {\n    type = &quot;the weekend&quot;;\n    adv = &quot;it's time to have some fun&quot;;\n  }\n  res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, { dayType: type, advice: adv });\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting the .ejs file anywhere other than the views folder, so render cannot find it.</li><li>Using res.send or res.sendFile for a template, which would not process the tags.</li><li>Mismatch between the key in the render object and the variable name used in the template, which causes a ReferenceError.</li><li>Forgetting &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; when using import syntax.</li><li>Instructor says there are many valid ways (if/else, switch, lookup object); any that passes the data correctly is fine.</li><li>Reference repo diverges slightly: its index.ejs variant uses 'weekday'/'work hard' wording and has an author footer, which should be ignored.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l210", "<p class=\"summary\">Surveys the EJS tag types (output, scriptlet, unescaped output, escape, comment, include) and then has the learner complete a template that uses them with a title, a seconds value, an array, raw HTML and a footer partial.</p>\n<h3>Anatomy of an EJS tag</h3>\n<ul><li>Every tag opens with &lt;% and normally closes with %&gt;. The character right after the opening &lt;% selects the behaviour.</li><li>Variants covered: &lt;%= (output), &lt;% (plain scriptlet), &lt;%- (unescaped output), &lt;%% (literal), &lt;%# (comment), and &lt;%- include(...) (insert another file).</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;%= value %&gt; : escaped output</h3>\n<ul><li>Contents are evaluated as JavaScript and the result is written into the page at that position, e.g. a variable holding a name.</li><li>The value is HTML-escaped, so if the string contains markup it shows up as visible text rather than being parsed.</li><li>This is the tag for anything displayed: titles, numbers, list item text.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;% code %&gt; : scriptlet, no output</h3>\n<ul><li>Plain tag with no modifier runs JavaScript only; nothing is written to the page. Good for if/else, loops, variable declarations, even console.log (which prints in the server terminal, since rendering happens on the server).</li><li>Control-flow constructs can be split across several tags, with plain HTML sitting between them. The HTML between tags is emitted only when the surrounding JavaScript lets execution reach it.</li><li>Rule: every line of JavaScript needs its own opening and closing tag, including lines holding only a closing curly brace or '})'. One tag cannot wrap a region that also contains HTML elements.</li><li>Forgetting to wrap an if line in tags makes it appear as literal text on the page.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;%- value %&gt; : unescaped output</h3>\n<ul><li>Writes the value as raw HTML, so '&lt;strong&gt;text&lt;/strong&gt;' sent from the server renders as bold text instead of showing the tag characters.</li><li>Swapping the minus for = shows the markup characters as plain text. The two tags differ only in escaping.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Only use &lt;%- with trusted content; with user input it opens the door to XSS.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;%% : escape tag</h3>\n<ul><li>A doubled percent in the opening (or closing) delimiter makes EJS print the delimiter as text rather than interpret it. Rarely needed, e.g. a page that documents EJS syntax.</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;%# comment %&gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>A hash after the opening delimiter creates an EJS comment: not executed, not rendered, and not sent to the browser (unlike an HTML comment, which still appears in page source).</li></ul>\n<h3>&lt;%- include('file.ejs') %&gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>Pastes another EJS file at that point. Argument is the filename in parentheses; the included file must also sit in the views folder (relative paths work from the including file).</li><li>Use it for repeated pieces such as header and footer so only the changing body is written per page. Layouts and partials get a full treatment in a later lesson.</li><li>The unescaped (-) form is required so the included HTML is not escaped.</li></ul>\n<h3>Worked example: looping an array</h3>\n<ul><li>Server passes an array (fruits). In the template a scriptlet tag starts a loop over it and another closes it; within each pass an li contains an output tag with the current element.</li><li>Result is an unordered list in array order.</li></ul>\n<h3>Exercise server setup</h3>\n<ul><li>Starter index.js already has a route that renders index.ejs with an object containing title, seconds (from the current clock time), items (array of three fruit names), and htmlContent (a string with HTML tags).</li><li>A footer.ejs partial exists in views.</li><li>Workflow: cd into the extracted folder, npm i to install modules, run with nodemon (auto-restarts on save) and refresh the browser. Running solution.js shows the target.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Tag cheat sheet</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;%= name %&gt;            &lt;!-- run JS, output escaped --&gt;\n&lt;%  console.log(1) %&gt;  &lt;!-- run JS, no output --&gt;\n&lt;%- html %&gt;            &lt;!-- output raw HTML --&gt;\n&lt;%% literal %%&gt;        &lt;!-- prints the delimiters as text --&gt;\n&lt;%# a comment %&gt;       &lt;!-- ignored entirely --&gt;\n&lt;%- include('footer.ejs') %&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Server route sending the data object</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const data = {\n    title: &quot;EJS Tags&quot;,\n    seconds: new Date().getSeconds(),\n    items: [&quot;apple&quot;, &quot;banana&quot;, &quot;cherry&quot;],\n    htmlContent: &quot;&lt;em&gt;This is some em text&lt;/em&gt;&quot;,\n  };\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, data);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Template body that satisfies the exercise</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;&lt;%= title %&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Current second: &lt;%= seconds %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;% if (seconds % 2 === 0) { %&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;% items.forEach((fruit) =&gt; { %&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;%= fruit %&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;% }) %&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;% } else { %&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;No items to display&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;% } %&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;%- htmlContent %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;%- include(&quot;footer.ejs&quot;) %&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Complete index.ejs using the different EJS tags</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Extracted lesson folder with index.js (route already written and sending title, seconds, items, htmlContent), views/index.ejs with placeholders and comments, views/footer.ejs, and solution files. Run npm i first.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Page shows the title in an h1, the current second in a paragraph, a fruit list when the second is even or 'No items to display' when it is odd, the htmlContent rendered as real HTML, and the footer pulled in via include.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the extracted folder, npm i, then start with nodemon index.js and open localhost:3000. Optionally run solution.js first to see the target.</li><li>Put the title variable inside the h1 with an output tag.</li><li>Put seconds in the paragraph with an output tag.</li><li>Turn the if/else commentary into real code: if (seconds % 2 === 0) show the ul, else show the 'No items to display' paragraph. Each line of JavaScript, including the closing braces, gets its own scriptlet tag; HTML stays outside tags.</li><li>Inside the ul loop over items (classic for loop with index or items.forEach((fruit) =&gt; ...)). Open the loop in one scriptlet tag, close it in another, and output each element with &lt;%= %&gt; inside an li. Without the output tag the literal word of the variable appears.</li><li>Render htmlContent with the unescaped tag &lt;%- %&gt; so the markup is interpreted. Compare what the = variant prints.</li><li>Add &lt;%- include('footer.ejs') %&gt; at the bottom.</li><li>Refresh repeatedly to hit both even and odd seconds and verify both branches.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Even second: heading, second count, bulleted list of apple, banana, cherry, formatted HTML text, footer. Odd second: same but with the 'No items to display' message instead of the list.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;h1&gt;&lt;%= title %&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Current second: &lt;%= seconds %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;% if (seconds % 2 === 0) { %&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;% items.forEach((fruit) =&gt; { %&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;%= fruit %&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;% }) %&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;% } else { %&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;No items to display&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;% } %&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;%- htmlContent %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;%- include(&quot;footer.ejs&quot;) %&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting that every JavaScript line, even a lone closing brace, needs its own tag.</li><li>Using the plain scriptlet tag where output is needed, so the loop variable never appears.</li><li>Using &lt;%= for HTML content and seeing tags as text; or the reverse with untrusted data.</li><li>Putting footer.ejs outside the views folder so the include fails.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: its starter index.ejs holds a finished solution, htmlContent uses strong/em, and the footer carries a personal credit line to be removed.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l211", "<p class=\"summary\">Reviews data flow in both directions with EJS (server to template via res.render, template to server via a POST form), explains why a missing variable crashes a template and how the locals object avoids that, then has the learner build a name-letter counter.</p>\n<h3>Server to template</h3>\n<ul><li>res.render(file, data) hands an object to the template. Each property name becomes a variable accessible in the EJS file and is printed with an output tag.</li><li>Arrays and other structures pass the same way and are iterated inside the template with a for or forEach loop wrapped in scriptlet tags.</li></ul>\n<h3>When the data is missing</h3>\n<ul><li>If the route forgets to pass a variable (bug, corrupted data, first page load) a template that references it throws a ReferenceError and the whole page fails to render.</li><li>A JavaScript-style guard such as if (fruits) does not help, because EJS compiles the template so that an undeclared name is simply an unresolved identifier; reading it throws before the check can be false.</li><li>Fix: read it through locals. locals is an object always present in the template that holds everything passed to render, so locals.fruits is just undefined when absent and the if safely evaluates false.</li><li>Plain name access and locals.name are the same data; locals.name is the safe-existence form.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> res.locals is the Express object for values shared by every render in a request; app.locals is app-wide. EJS merges them with the render data into locals.</li></ul>\n<h3>Template to server</h3>\n<ul><li>Data returns to the server through a standard HTML form: action is the route path, method is POST.</li><li>Each input's name attribute becomes the key in req.body, so name=&quot;fName&quot; is read as req.body.fName (or req.body['fName']).</li><li>req.body is only populated when body-parser (urlencoded) middleware is registered: app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })).</li><li>Inputs can use placeholder text instead of labels in this exercise.</li><li>The submit route must send a response. If it only computes and logs, the browser hangs on the request.</li></ul>\n<h3>Debugging tips from the demo</h3>\n<ul><li>console.log the length value, or req.body itself, to confirm what the form sent before rendering.</li><li>If behaviour looks stale, refresh the page, revisit the URL or restart the server (the form may have been loaded from an older version).</li><li>Error page output points at the template line number of the failing expression.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Server for the exercise</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;); // no data on first visit\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const numLetters = req.body[&quot;fName&quot;].length + req.body[&quot;lName&quot;].length;\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { numberOfLetters: numLetters });\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.ejs using locals to guard the variable</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;% if (locals.numberOfLetters) { %&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;There are &lt;%= numberOfLetters %&gt; letters in your name.&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;% } else { %&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Enter your name below&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;% } %&gt;\n  &lt;form action=&quot;/submit&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;fName&quot; placeholder=&quot;First name&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;lName&quot; placeholder=&quot;Last name&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;OK&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Crash vs safe check</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;% if (fruits) { %&gt;      &lt;!-- ReferenceError if render() did not pass fruits --&gt;\n&lt;% if (locals.fruits) { %&gt; &lt;!-- safe: undefined when absent --&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Name letter counter</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Extracted folder with index.js (body-parser already imported and configured, GET and POST routes stubbed) and views/index.ejs containing a form with two text inputs fName and lName posting to /submit. Run npm i. Run solution.js with nodemon to see the target.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> First visit shows an instruction h1. After submitting a first and last name, the page re-renders with an h1 reporting how many letters the two names contain in total, and the form is still shown.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run npm i, then nodemon solution.js and try it to see the behaviour; stop it afterwards.</li><li>Add an h1 asking for the name above the form in index.ejs.</li><li>In the GET route call res.render('index.ejs') and run nodemon index.js to check.</li><li>In the POST /submit route read req.body.fName and req.body.lName, compute the combined length, and console.log it to verify (test with a known name).</li><li>Call res.render('index.ejs') again with an object passing the count as numberOfLetters.</li><li>In index.ejs add an if/else with scriptlet tags: if the count exists, show an h1 with the count via an output tag; otherwise show the instruction h1.</li><li>Reload: it will crash with ReferenceError on the first visit because the variable is undefined. Change the condition to locals.numberOfLetters.</li><li>Verify both states: initial instruction and result after submission.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Initial page: 'Enter your name below'. After submitting e.g. two names with 8 letters total: 'There are 8 letters in your name.'</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;); // no data on first visit\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const numLetters = req.body[&quot;fName&quot;].length + req.body[&quot;lName&quot;].length;\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { numberOfLetters: numLetters });\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);\n});\n\n// views/solution.ejs body (condition uses locals):\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;% if (locals.numberOfLetters) { %&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;There are &lt;%= numberOfLetters %&gt; letters in your name.&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;% } else { %&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Enter your name below&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;% } %&gt;\n  &lt;form action=&quot;/submit&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;fName&quot; placeholder=&quot;First name&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; name=&quot;lName&quot; placeholder=&quot;Last name&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;OK&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Referencing a variable directly in an if condition when the route may not pass it: use locals.name.</li><li>Forgetting body-parser, leaving req.body undefined.</li><li>Not rendering or sending a response in the POST route, so the browser spins.</li><li>Mismatch between input name attributes and the keys read from req.body.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A truthiness test on locals.numberOfLetters treats 0 as missing; use locals.numberOfLetters !== undefined if zero must be shown.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: its index.ejs tests locals.numberOfLetters == 0 with the branches in the opposite order, which on the first visit evaluates false and prints 'There are undefined letters'; prefer the solution.ejs form. Both files carry an author footer to omit.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l212", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why static assets (CSS, images) need a public folder served by express.static, and how EJS partials pulled in with the include tag remove repeated header/footer markup across pages. Ends with a four-step exercise that builds a three-page site (home, about, contact) with shared partials.</p>\n<h3>Static files vs dynamic files</h3>\n<ul><li>Styling a server-rendered site means adding stylesheets, Bootstrap, icon fonts, a navbar and so on into the head of the EJS templates. Just linking local CSS in an EJS file does not work by default.</li><li>Reason: Express treats EJS views as dynamic (they are rendered into HTML at request time), while CSS, images, favicons and client-side JS never change on the server, so they are static files that Express must be told to serve as-is.</li><li>Convention in Node/Express projects: a folder named public at the top level of the project holds every static asset (public/styles/..., public/images/...).</li><li>Register it with the built-in middleware: app.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;)). The argument is the folder path relative to where the server is launched. If the folder lived elsewhere (for example src/public) the path string changes accordingly.</li><li>Once registered, everything inside public is served from the site root. The word public is NOT part of the URL: public/styles/layout.css is linked as /styles/layout.css (href=&quot;/styles/layout.css&quot;).</li><li>In other words link paths for static files are written relative to the public folder, not relative to the EJS file.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> express.static only serves files that exist; a missing file falls through to the next route and returns a 404, so a broken style link usually means the path or folder name is off by one segment.</li></ul>\n<h3>Routes are how pages are reached</h3>\n<ul><li>Dynamic pages are not found by file path. The navbar links (href=&quot;/about&quot;, href=&quot;/contact&quot;) make the browser send a GET request to that route, and the server must have an app.get handler for it that calls res.render.</li><li>A link click is effectively a GET request. A multi-page site is therefore just one app.get handler per page.</li><li>res.render(&quot;about.ejs&quot;) resolves the file relative to the views folder. If a template lived in views/main/, the call would be res.render(&quot;main/index.ejs&quot;).</li></ul>\n<h3>Partials with include</h3>\n<ul><li>Multi-page sites repeat the same head, navbar and footer on every page; only the main content changes. Partials let that shared markup live in one file.</li><li>Syntax: &lt;%- include(&quot;partials/header.ejs&quot;) %&gt;. The tag is the unescaped-output scriptlet (&lt;%-) followed by the function name include (singular, not includes) and the path in quotes.</li><li>&lt;%- (with a minus) is used because the included output is HTML that must be inserted raw. The escaping tag &lt;%= would turn the markup into visible text.</li><li>The path given to include is also relative to the views folder, so views/partials/header.ejs is written partials/header.ejs, the same rule as for res.render.</li><li>Typical layout: header include at the very top of each page template, page content in the middle, footer include at the end. Each page file then only contains what is unique to it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference code the header partial contains the doctype, html and head tags, the navbar and the opening main tag; the footer partial closes main, holds the footer markup and scripts, and closes body and html. The two partials are therefore a matched pair that wrap the page content, so they cannot be used separately.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Partials can also receive local data as a second argument, include(&quot;partials/header.ejs&quot;, { title: &quot;About&quot; }), which is useful for per-page titles.</li></ul>\n<h3>Project setup workflow</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the lesson zip, run npm i in the folder to install the packages listed in package.json, then start the finished version with nodemon solution.js to see the target at localhost:3000.</li><li>Work in index.js (the starter file). The finished reference is solution.js; compare only after trying.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Serving the public folder and a route per page</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\n// every file in ./public is now served from the site root\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/about&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;about.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/contact&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;contact.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">A page template wrapped by header and footer partials</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;%- include(&quot;partials/header.ejs&quot;) %&gt;\n\n&lt;h1&gt;About Me&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Page-specific content only.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;%- include(&quot;partials/footer.ejs&quot;) %&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Static asset links in the header partial (paths start at the public folder's contents)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;/styles/layout.css&quot;&gt;\n&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;/styles/content.css&quot;&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Project layout</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">project/\n  index.js\n  package.json\n  public/\n    images/cat.jpeg\n    styles/layout.css\n    styles/content.css\n  views/\n    index.ejs\n    about.ejs\n    contact.ejs\n    partials/\n      header.ejs\n      footer.ejs</pre>\n<h2>The task: 4.3 EJS Partials: fix the multi-page portfolio</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Zip with index.js (empty route section with comments), a views folder containing index.ejs (already using the partials), about.ejs, contact.ejs, partials/header.ejs and partials/footer.ejs, and a public folder with styles and an image. Run npm i, then nodemon solution.js to preview the target. In the starter, the about and contact pages are broken/unstyled.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Home, About and Contact pages all render with the same styled navbar, footer and CSS.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Step 1: add a GET handler for &quot;/&quot; that renders index.ejs.</li><li>Step 2: tell Express where static files live with app.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;)) so the CSS and images load.</li><li>Step 3: read the navbar hrefs in header.ejs (/about and /contact) and add a GET route for each that renders about.ejs and contact.ejs.</li><li>Step 4: in about.ejs and contact.ejs add the header include at the top and the footer include at the bottom, using paths relative to views (partials/header.ejs, partials/footer.ejs).</li><li>Run with nodemon index.js and click through the nav links to confirm all three pages look identical in layout.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> All three pages show the animated navbar, the wave footer and styling; navigating via the nav links works without 404s.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/about&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;about.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/contact&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;contact.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting express.static, so the page renders as plain unstyled HTML.</li><li>Writing /public/styles/layout.css in the href; the public folder name is not part of the URL.</li><li>Using &lt;%= include(...) %&gt; (escaped) instead of &lt;%- include(...) %&gt;, which prints the HTML as text.</li><li>Writing includes instead of include, or a path that is relative to the current file rather than to views.</li><li>Adding the header partial but forgetting the footer; the header opens main and html/body tags that the footer closes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's footer contains a personal credit line by the student author; omit it when copying the partial.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l213", "<p class=\"summary\">Module project: a styled Band Name Generator built with Express and EJS. A POST route picks a random adjective and noun, passes them to the template, a conditional switches the heading, shared partials carry the CSS link and footer, and the footer shows the current year computed on the server.</p>\n<h3>Project setup and target behaviour</h3>\n<ul><li>Download and unzip the starter, run npm i, then launch the finished version with nodemon solution.js to view the target at localhost:3000.</li><li>Target: the home page shows a default welcome h1 and a Generate Name button. Each click posts a form and the page comes back with a new 'adjective noun' heading. The page is styled and its footer shows a copyright with the current year.</li><li>The starter index.js lists four numbered steps with hints. The work is split across index.js, views/index.ejs and the two partials.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1: render the home page</h3>\n<ul><li>app.get(&quot;/&quot;) responds with res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;). Test after every step: the default h1 and the button should appear.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: random name generation and conditional heading</h3>\n<ul><li>The button lives inside a form with action=&quot;/submit&quot; and method=&quot;POST&quot;, so clicking it sends a POST to /submit. body-parser (app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }))) is already wired in; this form carries no fields, so it is not strictly needed here.</li><li>Random pick from an array: arr[Math.floor(Math.random() * arr.length)]. Math.random() returns a decimal in [0, 1), multiplying by the length scales it to the index range, Math.floor drops the decimals to a valid integer index.</li><li>Do this once for the adj array and once for the noun array (both are long word lists at the bottom of the file).</li><li>Send the result to the template with local data: res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, { adjective: randomAdj, noun: randomNoun }). The object's keys become variable names inside the EJS file.</li><li>In the template, print them with the escaped output tag: &lt;%= adjective %&gt; &lt;%= noun %&gt;, with a space between so the heading reads 'adjective noun'.</li><li>On first page load (GET) no data is passed, so referencing a bare adjective variable would throw a ReferenceError. Guard with a conditional using the scriptlet tag &lt;% ... %&gt;: if (locals.adjective &amp;&amp; locals.noun) render the generated heading, else render the default heading.</li><li>locals is the object holding everything passed to render. Reading a property on it returns undefined instead of throwing, which makes it the safe way to test whether data was supplied.</li><li>Each line of JavaScript control flow needs its own EJS tag: &lt;% if (...) { %&gt;, &lt;% } else { %&gt;, &lt;% } %&gt;. HTML written between the tags is output only for the branch that runs.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's starter version of the file wraps the generator in a helper function and passes a single bandName key instead of two keys; the lecture's own version passes adjective and noun separately.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: make the styling show up</h3>\n<ul><li>CSS is a static file: place it at public/styles/main.css and register the folder with app.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;)).</li><li>The stylesheet link goes in the head of the header partial. Its href is written from the point of view of the public folder: styles/main.css (a leading slash, /styles/main.css, is the safer form).</li><li>Pages must actually use the partials: add &lt;%- include(&quot;partials/header.ejs&quot;) %&gt; at the top of index.ejs and &lt;%- include(&quot;partials/footer.ejs&quot;) %&gt; at the bottom. Include paths are relative to the views folder.</li><li>Verify styling appears on both the initial GET page and the page rendered after the POST.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: dynamic year in the footer</h3>\n<ul><li>Hard-coded years in a copyright line go stale. Replace the placeholder year in the footer partial with &lt;%= new Date().getFullYear() %&gt;.</li><li>new Date() builds a Date for now and getFullYear() returns its four-digit year as a number. The output tag &lt;%= %&gt; prints the value into the page, while a plain &lt;% %&gt; tag would run the code and print nothing.</li><li>Because the code runs on the server each time the template is rendered, no client-side script is required.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Server: routes, random pick, passing data to the template</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const randomAdj = adj[Math.floor(Math.random() * adj.length)];\n  const randomNoun = noun[Math.floor(Math.random() * noun.length)];\n  res.render(&quot;index.ejs&quot;, {\n    adjective: randomAdj,\n    noun: randomNoun,\n  });\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Listening on port ${port}`);\n});\n\nconst adj = [&quot;able&quot;, &quot;adventurous&quot;, &quot;boiling&quot; /* ... long list ... */];\nconst noun = [&quot;hamburger&quot;, &quot;gerbil&quot;, &quot;oak&quot; /* ... long list ... */];</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">views/index.ejs: partials, conditional heading, form</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;%- include(&quot;partials/header.ejs&quot;) %&gt;\n\n&lt;% if (locals.adjective &amp;&amp; locals.noun) { %&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;&lt;%= adjective %&gt; &lt;%= noun %&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;% } else { %&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to the Band Generator&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;% } %&gt;\n\n&lt;form action=&quot;/submit&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;submit&quot; value=&quot;Generate Name&quot;&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n\n&lt;%- include(&quot;partials/footer.ejs&quot;) %&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">views/partials/header.ejs (opens html, head, body and main)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;UTF-8&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;/styles/main.css&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Band Generator&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">views/partials/footer.ejs (closes main, body and html)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">  &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Copyright &amp;copy; &lt;%= new Date().getFullYear() %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Band Name Generator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Zip containing index.js (routes missing, four commented steps with hints, adjective and noun arrays at the bottom), views/index.ejs and partials header.ejs/footer.ejs with comments marking where code goes, public/styles/main.css, and a finished solution.js with solution.ejs.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A fully functional, styled generator: default heading on first visit, a random 'adjective noun' heading after each button click, and a footer with the current year.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Step 1: add the GET / route that renders index.ejs.</li><li>Step 2: in the POST /submit route pick a random adjective and noun from the arrays, then render index.ejs with those values. In index.ejs show them in the h1 when present and show the default h1 otherwise.</li><li>Step 3: serve the public folder with express.static, link styles/main.css inside the header partial, and include the header and footer partials in index.ejs.</li><li>Step 4: replace the placeholder year in the footer partial with a server-computed current year using new Date().getFullYear().</li><li>Test each step in the browser at localhost:3000 before moving to the next.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Visiting / shows the styled welcome heading and button. Clicking Generate Name shows a new random band name each time. The footer reads Copyright followed by the current year.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const randomAdj = adj[Math.floor(Math.random() * adj.length)];\n  const randomNoun = noun[Math.floor(Math.random() * noun.length)];\n  res.render(&quot;solution.ejs&quot;, {\n    adjective: randomAdj,\n    noun: randomNoun,\n  });\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Accessing adjective or noun without the locals guard, which throws a ReferenceError on the initial GET.</li><li>Using &lt;% %&gt; where &lt;%= %&gt; is needed (nothing printed) or &lt;%= %&gt; for include (HTML escaped).</li><li>Forgetting the stylesheet link in the header partial, or not including the partials in index.ejs, so no styling shows.</li><li>Index maths: Math.floor(Math.random() * arr.length) must use the array length; using length - 1 would exclude the last item.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The starter repo's index.js lists duplicate words in the arrays, and the student's footer partial carries a personal credit line that should be dropped when reusing it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Rendering directly from a POST means a browser refresh asks to resubmit the form; a redirect pattern avoids that but then needs another way to carry the data.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l214", "<p class=\"summary\">A short learning-habits talk about the gap between following a tutorial and building alone, and the confidence curve most learners go through. No code is covered.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Understanding a demo while watching is not the same as being able to reproduce it. Skill comes from doing the work yourself.</li><li>Suggested routine: watch for the overall idea, jot down function names and tools you may need, then pause the video and attempt the exercise on your own before seeing the solution.</li><li>Early on, new learners often overestimate what they know (the Dunning-Kruger effect). As knowledge grows, the gaps become visible and confidence drops into a low point, often experienced as impostor syndrome.</li><li>Both extremes are distortions; the realistic position is somewhere between. Consistent practice and applying what is learned is what moves you forward.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Treating passive video watching as learning and skipping the pause-and-try step.</li></ul>");
